#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:6
msgid "A C Preprocessor Macro Example"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:8
msgid "C and C++ macros perform literal, raw text replacement."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:10
msgid ""
"This allows developers substantial freedom to write C or C++ programs as "
"sequences of tokens that look little like normal code before preprocessing:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:14
msgid "<iostream>"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:30
msgid "\"Uh-Oh!\""
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:35
msgid "Key takeaways from this example:"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:37
msgid ""
"**Custom Syntax:** Using `BEGIN` and `END` in place of braces is not "
"permitted by C itself, and would surprise most readers, but as the "
"preprocessing runs before compilation, the compiler just sees the braces to "
"which these macros expand."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:41
msgid ""
"**Unhygienic State Access:** The `EXPECT` macro implicitly accesses `idx`, "
"which is defined in `main` and not passed into the macro. This makes the "
"macro's effect depend on the context in which it is invoked (e.g., suppose "
"`idx` was a pointer to a `struct BTreeIndex`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:48
msgid ""
"Explain the C preprocessor operates as a pure copy-paste system. It replaces "
"tokens line by line without any syntactic structure check."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:50
msgid ""
"Notice how `EXPECT(< 10)` expands to `if (idx < 10) {}`. The macro assumes "
"`idx` exists and is accessible."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/intro/cpp-example.md:52
msgid ""
"If someone uses `EXPECT` inside a function without `idx`, they get a "
"compiler error at the _expansion site_, not the macro definition site, which "
"can lead to confusing and hard-to-debug compiler errors."
msgstr ""
